<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="u6-constraints-svd" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Constrained optimization and Lagrange multipliers</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="def-8-1-optimization"/> that an optimization problem has an objective function and a feasible region. In a constrained optimization problem, one or more equations or inequalities restrict the feasible region.
    </p>
    <p>
      <xref ref="sec-maxima-minima-problems" text="custom">The section “Optimization problems and critical points”</xref> found interior critical points by solving
      <me>
        \nabla f(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{0}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      On a closed bounded region, absolute extrema may also occur on the boundary. Lagrange multipliers give equations for critical points on boundaries and feasible sets described by one or more equality constraints.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same method connects constrained optimization to linear algebra. On the unit sphere, the extrema of a symmetric quadratic form occur in eigenvector directions. This leads to <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis" text="custom">the section “Principal component analysis”</xref> for centered data and <xref ref="sec-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">the section “Singular value decomposition”</xref> for general matrix maps.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Big questions.</em> How do we find absolute extrema when the allowable inputs are restricted? Why do eigenvectors identify extremal directions of quadratic forms, data sets, and matrix maps?
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Learning outcomes.</em> By the end of this unit, students should be able to:
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Core outcomes.</em>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO1.</em> Find absolute extrema on closed bounded regions by comparing interior and boundary candidates.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO2.</em> Formulate and solve Lagrange-multiplier problems with one equality constraint, and explain the parallel-gradient and Lagrangian formulations.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO3.</em> Formulate and solve Lagrange-multiplier problems with several equality constraints, and interpret the objective gradient as a linear combination of constraint gradients.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO4.</em> Use Lagrange multipliers and eigenvalues to find extrema of symmetric quadratic forms on the unit sphere, and apply this result to maximum stretch and singular values.</p></li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO5.</em> Center a small data set, form its covariance matrix, compute and interpret first and second principal directions, scores, reconstructions, and captured variation, and distinguish PCA from linear regression.</p></li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>U6-LO6.</em> Construct and interpret a small singular value decomposition
          <me>
            A=U\Sigma V^T,
          </me>
          including its orthonormal input and output directions and singular-value stretch factors.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li><p><em>U6-LO7.</em> Use an SVD to identify rank and orthonormal bases for the four fundamental subspaces, and connect the SVD of centered data with PCA directions, scores, and reconstructions.</p></li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <em>Toolbox skills.</em> Compare interior and boundary candidates; restrict a function to simple boundary pieces; form and solve one- and several-constraint Lagrange systems; write and differentiate Lagrangians; evaluate and diagonalize symmetric quadratic forms; center data and form covariance matrices; compute principal scores, projections, and reconstruction residuals; form <m>A^TA</m>; compute singular values and singular vectors; assemble and read small SVDs; and identify rank and bases for the four fundamental subspaces.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Application and computation outcomes.</em> Interpret <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u6">Lab U6: Constraints, PCA, and SVD</url> <c>[U6-LO2, U6-LO3, U6-LO4, U6-LO5, U6-LO6, U6-LO7 | C+R+M+T | Core]</c> by reading one- and several-constraint Lagrange checks, maximum-stretch calculations, centered data and covariance matrices, PCA scores and reconstructions, PCA–regression comparisons, PCA–SVD identities, and SVD rank and fundamental-subspace checks.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-absolute-extrema.ptx" />

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-lagrange-multipliers.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-maximum-stretch-svd">
    <title>Quadratic forms and maximum stretch</title>
    <p>
      Recall from <xref ref="def-u5-quadratic-form"/> that an <m>n\times n</m> matrix <m>B</m> determines the scalar-valued function
      <me>
        q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
      </me>
      In this section, <m>B</m> will be symmetric. By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>, <m>B</m> has an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. Lagrange multipliers give these eigenvectors an optimization interpretation.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors">
      <title>Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>B</m> be a symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrix, and let
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          If <m>\lambda_{\min}</m> and <m>\lambda_{\max}</m> are the smallest and largest eigenvalues of <m>B</m>, then
          <me>
            \min_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})
            =
            \lambda_{\min}
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})
            =
            \lambda_{\max}.
          </me>
          These values are achieved at unit eigenvectors for <m>\lambda_{\min}</m> and <m>\lambda_{\max}</m>. More generally, every constrained critical point is a unit eigenvector of <m>B</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Use the constraint
          <me>
            g(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^T\mathbf{x}=1.
          </me>
          Since <m>B</m> is symmetric,
          <me>
            \nabla q_B(\mathbf{x})=2B\mathbf{x},
            \qquad
            \nabla g(\mathbf{x})=2\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          On the unit sphere, <m>\nabla g(\mathbf{x})\neq\mathbf{0}</m>, so <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier"/> applies. Its equations are
          <me>
            2B\mathbf{x}=2\mu\mathbf{x},
          </me>
          or
          <me>
            B\mathbf{x}=\mu\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
          Thus the constrained critical points are unit eigenvectors of <m>B</m>. In this calculation, the Lagrange multiplier <m>\mu</m> is the corresponding eigenvalue.
        </p>
        <p>
          If <m>B\mathbf{x}=\lambda\mathbf{x}</m> and <m>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1</m>, then
          <md>
            <mrow>q_B(\mathbf{x})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^T(\lambda\mathbf{x})</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda\|\mathbf{x}\|^2</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The unit sphere is closed and bounded, so absolute extrema exist by <xref ref="thm-u6-extreme-value"/>. Comparing the eigenvalues gives the smallest and largest values.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-principal-coordinate-view">
      <title>Principal-coordinate view</title>
      <p>
        The Lagrange calculation identifies the critical directions. Orthogonal diagonalization explains all possible values of the quadratic form.
      </p>
      <p>
        By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>, write
        <me>
          B=QDQ^T,
          \qquad
          D=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \lambda_1 \amp \amp 0\\
          \amp \ddots \amp\\
          0 \amp \amp \lambda_n
          \end{bmatrix},
        </me>
        where the columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal eigenvectors of <m>B</m>. Set
        <me>
          \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x}.
        </me>
        Since <m>Q</m> is orthogonal, <xref ref="lem-orthogonal-matrix-properties"/> gives
        <me>
          \|\mathbf{c}\|=\|\mathbf{x}\|.
        </me>
        Also,
        <md>
          <mrow>q_B(\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{c}^TD\mathbf{c}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\lambda_1c_1^2+\cdots+\lambda_nc_n^2.</mrow>
        </md>
        On the unit sphere,
        <me>
          c_1^2+\cdots+c_n^2=1.
        </me>
        Thus <m>q_B(\mathbf{x})</m> is a weighted average of the eigenvalues, with nonnegative weights <m>c_i^2</m>. It must lie between the smallest and largest eigenvalues.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-non-diagonal-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
      <title>A non-diagonal quadratic form on the unit circle</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to the symmetric matrix from <xref ref="ex-symmetric-matrix-2x2"/>:
          <me>
            B=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x\\
            y
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Write <m>q_B(x,y)</m> in scalar form.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Set up the Lagrange equations for finding the extrema of <m>q_B</m> subject to
              <me>
                x^2+y^2=1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Find all constrained critical points and their <m>q_B</m>-values.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find an orthogonal matrix <m>Q</m> and diagonal matrix <m>D</m> such that
              <me>
                B=QDQ^T,
              </me>
              with the larger eigenvalue first.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If
              <me>
                \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x},
              </me>
              rewrite <m>q_B</m> in principal coordinates and use this form to identify its absolute maximum and minimum.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO4, U5-LO4 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-non-diagonal-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
        <p>
          We have
          <md>
            <mrow>q_B(x,y)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x \amp y
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp 2
            \end{bmatrix}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            x\\
            y
            \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=2x^2-2xy+2y^2.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          Its gradient is
          <me>
            \nabla q_B(x,y)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4x-2y\\
            -2x+4y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            g(x,y)=x^2+y^2,
          </me>
          we have
          <me>
            \nabla g(x,y)
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2x\\
            2y
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The Lagrange equations are therefore
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4x-2y\\
            -2x+4y
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \mu
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2x\\
            2y
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            x^2+y^2=1.
          </me>
          Dividing the gradient equation by <m>2</m> gives
          <me>
            B\mathbf{x}=\mu\mathbf{x}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The characteristic polynomial is
          <md>
            <mrow>\det(B-\mu I)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(2-\mu)^2-1</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(\mu-1)(\mu-3).</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mu=1</m>, the eigenvectors satisfy <m>x=y</m>. The corresponding unit vectors are
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          At either point,
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For <m>\mu=3</m>, the eigenvectors satisfy <m>x=-y</m>. The corresponding unit vectors are
          <me>
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            -\frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          At either point,
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=3.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Choose
          <me>
            Q=
            \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            -1 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            D=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The columns of <m>Q</m> are orthonormal eigenvectors of <m>B</m>, so
          <me>
            B=QDQ^T.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          If
          <me>
            \mathbf{c}=Q^T\mathbf{x},
          </me>
          then
          <me>
            q_B(\mathbf{x})=3c_1^2+c_2^2.
          </me>
          On the unit circle,
          <me>
            c_1^2+c_2^2=1.
          </me>
          Therefore the absolute maximum is <m>3</m>, attained when <m>c_1^2=1</m>, and the absolute minimum is <m>1</m>, attained when <m>c_2^2=1</m>. These are the same values and directions found from the Lagrange equations.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <paragraphs>
      <title>Maximum stretch of a matrix</title>
      <p>
        Now let <m>A</m> be an <m>m\times n</m> matrix. The matrix <m>A</m> need not be square or symmetric. One way to study the matrix map
        <me>
          \mathbf{x}\longmapsto A\mathbf{x}
        </me>
        is to ask which unit inputs produce the largest output lengths.
      </p>
      <p>
        Because lengths are nonnegative, maximizing <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m> is equivalent to maximizing its square. We have
        <md>
          <mrow>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=(A\mathbf{x})^T(A\mathbf{x})</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus maximum stretch is a quadratic-form problem for the symmetric matrix <m>A^TA</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> are nonnegative. Indeed, if
        <me>
          A^TA\mathbf{v}=\lambda\mathbf{v}
        </me>
        for a nonzero vector <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, then
        <md>
          <mrow>\lambda\|\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}^TA^TA\mathbf{v}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\|A\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\geq 0.</mrow>
        </md>
        Therefore <m>\lambda\geq 0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Order the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> from largest to smallest and take their nonnegative square roots:
        <me>
          \sigma_i=\sqrt{\lambda_i}.
        </me>
        These numbers are the singular values of <m>A</m>. A unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_i</m> of <m>A^TA</m> is a right singular vector of <m>A</m>. Since
        <md>
          <mrow>\|A\mathbf{v}_i\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}_i^TA^TA\mathbf{v}_i</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\lambda_i,</mrow>
        </md>
        we have
        <me>
          \|A\mathbf{v}_i\|=\sigma_i.
        </me>
        Thus the right singular vectors are special input directions, and the singular values are their stretch factors. In particular,
        <me>
          \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|
          =
          \sigma_1.
        </me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-small-svd-stretch">
      <title>A small stretch computation</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            A=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{e}_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{e}_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{e}_1</m> and <m>A\mathbf{e}_2</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>\|A\mathbf{e}_1\|</m> and <m>\|A\mathbf{e}_2\|</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Compute <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>What are the singular values of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which unit direction is stretched most?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            A\mathbf{e}_1=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            0
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            A\mathbf{e}_2=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            0\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \|A\mathbf{e}_1\|=3,
            \qquad
            \|A\mathbf{e}_2\|=1.
          </me>
          Also
          <me>
            A^TA=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}^T
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            9 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          The eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> are <m>9</m> and <m>1</m>. Therefore the singular values of <m>A</m> are
          <me>
            \sigma_1=3,
            \qquad
            \sigma_2=1.
          </me>
          The direction <m>\mathbf{e}_1</m> is stretched most. This is the simplest version of the question
          <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-reading-maximum-stretch-code">
      <title>Reading the same stretch in code</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The following code repeats the previous activity using <m>A^TA</m>.
        </p>
        <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([[3., 0.],
              [0., 1.]])

B = A.T @ A
eigvals, eigvecs = np.linalg.eigh(B)

singular_values = np.sqrt(eigvals[::-1])
dominant_direction = eigvecs[:, -1]

B, eigvals, singular_values, dominant_direction
]]></code></program>
        <p>Output:</p>
        <pre><![CDATA[(array([[9., 0.],
        [0., 1.]]),
 array([1., 9.]),
 array([3., 1.]),
 array([1., 0.]))]]></pre>
        <ol>
          <li><p>What mathematical matrix is stored in <c>B</c>?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why are the entries of <c>eigvals</c> squared stretch factors?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does the code take a square root?</p></li>
          <li><p>Why does <c>eigvecs[:, -1]</c> give the most-stretched input direction?</p></li>
          <li><p>What value of <m>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m> does the code predict?</p></li>
        </ol>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The matrix <c>B</c> is <m>A^TA</m>. The entries of <c>eigvals</c> are eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>, so they are values of <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</m> for special unit input directions. The code takes a square root because singular values measure <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|</m>, not <m>\|A\mathbf{x}\|^2</m>. The command <c>np.linalg.eigh</c> lists eigenvalues in increasing order, so the largest eigenvalue is last, and <c>eigvecs[:, -1]</c> is its eigenvector. The largest singular value is <m>3</m>, so
          <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|=3.</me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <p>
      The same quadratic-form problem appears when a matrix stores centered data. <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis"/> uses it to choose directions that capture variation in a data set.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis">
    <title>Principal component analysis</title>

    <p>
      A data matrix can have many feature columns. Principal component analysis, or PCA, looks for orthogonal directions that capture as much centered variation as possible. The first direction is found with one constraint: it must have unit length. Each later direction has additional orthogonality constraints.
    </p>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-centered-data-covariance">
      <title>Centered data and covariance matrix</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            \mathbf{x}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{x}_n\in\R^d
          </me>
          be data vectors. Their mean is
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n\mathbf{x}_i.
          </me>
          The centered data vectors are
          <me>
            \mathbf{z}_i
            =
            \mathbf{x}_i-\bar{\mathbf{x}}.
          </me>
          Let <m>Z</m> be the <m>n\times d</m> matrix whose <m>i</m>-th row is <m>\mathbf{z}_i^T</m>. The covariance matrix is
          <md>
            <mrow>C</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac1n Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n \mathbf{z}_i\mathbf{z}_i^T.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <p>
      Some conventions divide by <m>n-1</m> instead of <m>n</m>. This multiplies every covariance eigenvalue by the same positive constant and does not change the eigenvectors.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-scores-captured-variation">
      <title>Scores and captured variation</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>\mathbf{v}\in\R^d</m> be a unit vector. The score of the <m>i</m>-th centered data point in direction <m>\mathbf{v}</m> is
        <me>
          t_i
          =
          \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{z}_i.
        </me>
        The score vector is
        <me>
          \mathbf{t}=Z\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        Its average squared score is
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac1n\sum_{i=1}^n t_i^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\|Z\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Thus captured variation is a quadratic form.
      </p>
      <p>
        The matrix <m>C</m> is symmetric. Also,
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp=\frac1n\|Z\mathbf{v}\|^2</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp\geq 0</mrow>
        </md>
        for every <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, so the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> are nonnegative.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-first-principal-direction">
      <title>First principal direction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          A first principal direction is a unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> that maximizes
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
          </me>
          subject to
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{v}\|=1.
          </me>
          The entries of
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1
          </me>
          are the first principal-component scores.
        </p>
        <p>
          By <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors"/>, <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> may be chosen as a unit eigenvector of <m>C</m> corresponding to its largest eigenvalue <m>\lambda_1</m>. The maximum captured variation is <m>\lambda_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-reconstruction-view">
      <title>Reconstruction view</title>
      <p>
        For a unit direction <m>\mathbf{v}</m>, the orthogonal projection of <m>\mathbf{z}_i</m> onto
        <me>
          \spans\{\mathbf{v}\}
        </me>
        is
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i
          =
          t_i\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        The corresponding reconstructed data point is
        <me>
          \widehat{\mathbf{x}}_i
          =
          \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i.
        </me>
        Define the reconstruction residual by
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}_i
          =
          \mathbf{z}_i-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i.
        </me>
        By <xref ref="def-orthogonal-projection"/> and <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>,
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}_i\perp\mathbf{v}.
        </me>
        Therefore
        <me>
          \|\mathbf{z}_i\|^2
          =
          t_i^2+\|\mathbf{r}_i\|^2.
        </me>
        Summing over the data gives
        <me>
          \sum_{i=1}^n\|\mathbf{z}_i\|^2
          =
          \sum_{i=1}^n t_i^2
          +
          \sum_{i=1}^n\|\mathbf{r}_i\|^2.
        </me>
        The quantity on the left does not depend on <m>\mathbf{v}</m>. Thus maximizing the total squared scores is equivalent to minimizing the total squared reconstruction residuals.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-pca-small-data-set">
      <title>Principal directions of a small data set</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          The rows of
          <me>
            X=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            4 \amp 3\\
            3 \amp 4\\
            0 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 0
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          are four data points in <m>\R^2</m>.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><p>Compute the mean <m>\bar{\mathbf{x}}</m> and the centered data matrix <m>Z</m>.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute
              <me>
                C=\frac14Z^TZ.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors. Choose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> for the larger eigenvalue and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for the smaller eigenvalue.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the first principal-component score vector
              <me>
                \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              For the first centered point <m>\mathbf{z}_1</m>, compute
              <me>
                \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
                =
                (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)\mathbf{v}_1
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}_1
                =
                \mathbf{z}_1-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
              </me>
              Check that
              <me>
                \mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1=0
              </me>
              and
              <me>
                \|\mathbf{z}_1\|^2
                =
                (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)^2+\|\mathbf{r}_1\|^2.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Form
              <me>
                V=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \mathbf{v}_2
                \end{bmatrix}
              </me>
              and compute <m>V^TCV</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Describe the affine line
              <me>
                \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5, U5-LO4, U4-LO3 | P+C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-pca-small-data-set">
        <p>
          The mean is
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <me>
            Z=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 2\\
            -2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We calculate
          <md>
            <mrow>C</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac14Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac14
            \begin{bmatrix}
            10 \amp 8\\
            8 \amp 10
            \end{bmatrix}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            5/2 \amp 2\\
            2 \amp 5/2
            \end{bmatrix}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
        <p>
          The eigenvalues are
          <me>
            \lambda_1=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \lambda_2=\frac12.
          </me>
          Corresponding unit eigenvectors are
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{v}_2
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1
            =
            Z\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            3\\
            -3\\
            -3
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          For
          <me>
            \mathbf{z}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          the score is
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1
            =
            \frac3{\sqrt2}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
            =
            \frac3{\sqrt2}\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2\\
            3/2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1/2\\
            -1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            \mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1
            =
            0.
          </me>
          Also,
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{z}_1\|^2=5,
            \qquad
            (\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1)^2=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \|\mathbf{r}_1\|^2=\frac12,
          </me>
          so
          <me>
            5=\frac92+\frac12.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          With
          <me>
            V
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp -1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          we obtain
          <me>
            V^TCV
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            9/2 \amp 0\\
            0 \amp 1/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          The first direction captures the fraction
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{\lambda_1}{\lambda_1+\lambda_2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac{9/2}{9/2+1/2}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\frac9{10}</mrow>
          </md>
          of the total centered variation.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \bar{\mathbf{x}}
            +
            \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}
            =
            \left\{
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2\\
            2
            \end{bmatrix}
            +
            s
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}
            :
            s\in\R
            \right\}.
          </me>
          This is the line <m>y=x</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>

    <definition xml:id="def-u6-second-principal-direction">
      <title>Second principal direction</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is a first principal direction. A second principal direction is a unit vector <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> that maximizes
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
          </me>
          subject to the two constraints
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}=1
          </me>
          and
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}_1=0.
          </me>
          In <m>\R^2</m>, the second constraint leaves only one axis, up to sign. In higher dimensions, it restricts the optimization to the unit vectors orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </definition>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-second-principal-direction-eigenvector">
      <title>The second principal direction is another eigenvector</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> be ordered so that
          <me>
            \lambda_1\geq\lambda_2\geq\cdots\geq\lambda_d.
          </me>
          If <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is a unit eigenvector for <m>\lambda_1</m>, then a second principal direction may be chosen as a unit eigenvector <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for <m>\lambda_2</m> that is orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          On the feasible set, the gradients of the two constraints are
          <me>
            2\mathbf{v}
            \qquad\text{and}\qquad
            \mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
          They are nonzero and orthogonal, so they are linearly independent by <xref ref="thm-6-1-orthogonality-independent"/>. Thus <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier-several-constraints"/> applies.
        </p>
        <p>
          Define
          <me>
            L(\mathbf{v},\mu,\nu)
            =
            \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
            -
            \mu(\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}-1)
            -
            \nu\mathbf{v}^T\mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
          The stationarity equation is
          <me>
            2C\mathbf{v}
            =
            2\mu\mathbf{v}+\nu\mathbf{v}_1.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Take the dot product with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. Since <m>C</m> is symmetric,
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{v}_1^TC\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=(C\mathbf{v}_1)^T\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=\lambda_1\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{v}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=0.</mrow>
          </md>
          The first constraint term also has zero dot product with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. Since
          <me>
            \|\mathbf{v}_1\|=1,
          </me>
          it follows that <m>\nu=0</m>. Hence
          <me>
            C\mathbf{v}=\mu\mathbf{v}.
          </me>
          Thus a constrained critical direction is an eigenvector of <m>C</m>. Among the eigenvectors orthogonal to <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>, the largest remaining value of the quadratic form is <m>\lambda_2</m>.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <p>
      More generally, the <m>j</m>-th principal direction maximizes
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}^TC\mathbf{v}
      </me>
      subject to unit length and orthogonality to
      <me>
        \mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_{j-1}.
      </me>
      The same argument gives a unit eigenvector for <m>\lambda_j</m>. If an eigenvalue is repeated, the individual principal directions inside its eigenspace are not unique, but their span is fixed.
    </p>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-principal-coordinates">
      <title>Principal coordinates</title>
      <p>
        Collect an orthonormal set of principal directions as the columns of
        <me>
          V=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_d
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        By <xref ref="thm-principal-axes"/>,
        <me>
          V^TCV
          =
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \lambda_1 \amp \amp 0\\
          \amp \ddots \amp\\
          0 \amp \amp \lambda_d
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </me>
        The principal-coordinate matrix is
        <me>
          T=ZV.
        </me>
        Its <m>j</m>-th column contains the scores in direction <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m>, and
        <me>
          \frac1nT^TT
          =
          V^TCV.
        </me>
        Thus <m>\lambda_j</m> is the average squared score in the <m>j</m>-th principal direction, while the off-diagonal entries are zero.
      </p>
      <p>
        The total centered variation is
        <me>
          \lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d.
        </me>
        When the centered data are not all zero, the fraction captured by the first <m>k</m> principal directions is
        <me>
          \frac{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k}
          {\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d}.
        </me>
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-linear-regression">
      <title>PCA and linear regression</title>
      <p>
        PCA and linear regression both use projection and sums of squared residuals, but they solve different problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        PCA treats the feature coordinates symmetrically. It chooses an affine subspace through the data mean, and its reconstruction residuals are perpendicular to that subspace.
      </p>
      <p>
        Linear regression designates one coordinate as the response and minimizes vertical prediction residuals in the data plot. Algebraically, it projects the response vector onto the column space of a design matrix, as in <xref ref="sec-u4-least-squares-as-projection"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Thus PCA and linear regression can choose different best-fit lines for the same data.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <p>
      For PCA, the principal directions come from
      <me>
        C=\frac1nZ^TZ.
      </me>
      <xref ref="sec-singular-value-decomposition"/> packages the corresponding input directions, output directions, and stretch factors directly from <m>Z</m>, and extends the same picture to an arbitrary rectangular matrix.
    </p>
  </section>

  <xi:include href="sec-u6-singular-value-decomposition.ptx" />

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-svd-information-channels">
    <title>PCA from the SVD</title>

    <p>
      <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis"/> found principal directions by diagonalizing the covariance matrix
      <me>
        C=\frac1nZ^TZ.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      The SVD of the centered data matrix <m>Z</m> contains the same directions and the same captured-variation values. It also connects principal-component scores with left singular vectors.
    </p>

    <fact xml:id="fact-u6-pca-from-centered-data-svd">
      <title>PCA from a centered-data SVD</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let
          <me>
            Z\in\R^{n\times d}
          </me>
          be a centered data matrix, and let
          <me>
            Z=U\Sigma V^T
          </me>
          be <xref ref="def-u6-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">an SVD</xref>. Write
          <me>
            V=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_d
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and include zero singular values when needed. Then
          <me>
            C=\frac1nZ^TZ
            =
            V\left(\frac1n\Sigma^T\Sigma\right)V^T.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>Therefore:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>the right singular vectors <m>\mathbf{v}_j</m> are principal directions;</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the covariance eigenvalues are
              <me>
                \lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{n};
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              the principal-coordinate matrix is
              <me>
                T=ZV=U\Sigma.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In particular, the <m>j</m>-th score vector is
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_j=\sigma_j\mathbf{u}_j
          </me>
          when <m>\sigma_j&gt;0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <proof>
        <p>
          Since <m>U</m> is orthogonal,
          <me>
            U^TU=I.
          </me>
          Therefore
          <md>
            <mrow>Z^TZ</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            (U\Sigma V^T)^T(U\Sigma V^T)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            V\Sigma^TU^TU\Sigma V^T</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            V\Sigma^T\Sigma V^T.</mrow>
          </md>
          Dividing by <m>n</m> gives the displayed orthogonal diagonalization of <m>C</m>. The columns of <m>V</m> are therefore covariance eigenvectors, with eigenvalues <m>\sigma_j^2/n</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Also,
          <md>
            <mrow>ZV</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            U\Sigma V^TV</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp=
            U\Sigma.</mrow>
          </md>
          Thus the columns of <m>U\Sigma</m> are the principal-component score vectors.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </fact>

    <paragraphs xml:id="par-u6-pca-keeping-first-k-directions">
      <title>Keeping the first <m>k</m> principal directions</title>
      <p>
        Let <m>p=\min(n,d)</m> and <m>1\leq k\leq p</m>. Set
        <me>
          V_k=
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \mathbf{v}_1 \amp \cdots \amp \mathbf{v}_k
          \end{bmatrix},
          \qquad
          T_k=ZV_k,
        </me>
        where <m>V_k\in\R^{d\times k}</m> and <m>T_k\in\R^{n\times k}</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The centered-data reconstruction using the first <m>k</m> principal directions is
        <me>
          \widehat Z_k
          =
          T_kV_k^T
          =
          ZV_kV_k^T.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        Since the columns of <m>V_k</m> are orthonormal, each row of <m>\widehat Z_k</m> is the orthogonal projection of the corresponding centered data vector onto
        <me>
          \spans
          \{\mathbf{v}_1,\ldots,\mathbf{v}_k\};
        </me>
        see <xref ref="thm-6-2projection"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If <m>U_k\in\R^{n\times k}</m> contains the first <m>k</m> left singular vectors and
        <me>
          \Sigma_k
          =
          \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_1,\ldots,\sigma_k)
          \in\R^{k\times k},
        </me>
        then
        <me>
          \widehat Z_k
          =
          U_k\Sigma_kV_k^T.
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        The reconstruction has rank at most <m>k</m>. Each <m>d</m>-dimensional centered data point is represented by its <m>k</m> scores and reconstructed from those scores.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fraction of total centered variation captured is
        <me>
          \frac{\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_k}
          {\lambda_1+\cdots+\lambda_d}
          =
          \frac{\sigma_1^2+\cdots+\sigma_k^2}
          {\sigma_1^2+\cdots+\sigma_p^2},
        </me>
        provided the centered data are not all zero.
      </p>
    </paragraphs>

    <activity xml:id="act-u6-pca-small-data-set-through-svd">
      <title>The small PCA data set through the SVD</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Return to <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>. There,
          <me>
            Z=
            \begin{bmatrix}
            2 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp 2\\
            -2 \amp -1\\
            -1 \amp -2
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            V=
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1 \amp 1\\
            1 \amp -1
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          and the covariance eigenvalues are
          <me>
            \lambda_1=\frac92,
            \qquad
            \lambda_2=\frac12.
          </me>
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Use
              <me>
                \lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{4}
              </me>
              to find the singular values of <m>Z</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the principal-coordinate matrix
              <me>
                T=ZV.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>\mathbf{t}_1,\mathbf{t}_2</m> be the columns of <m>T</m>. Compute
              <me>
                \mathbf{u}_1=\frac{\mathbf{t}_1}{\sigma_1},
                \qquad
                \mathbf{u}_2=\frac{\mathbf{t}_2}{\sigma_2},
              </me>
              and verify that <m>\mathbf{u}_1,\mathbf{u}_2</m> are orthonormal.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Set
              <me>
                U_2=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{u}_1 \amp \mathbf{u}_2
                \end{bmatrix},
                \qquad
                \Sigma_2=
                \begin{bmatrix}
                \sigma_1 \amp 0\\
                0 \amp \sigma_2
                \end{bmatrix}.
              </me>
              Explain why
              <me>
                Z=U_2\Sigma_2V^T.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Compute the reconstruction using only the first principal direction:
              <me>
                \widehat Z_1
                =
                \mathbf{t}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T.
              </me>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li><p>Interpret the rows of <m>\widehat Z_1</m>. How does its first row compare with the projection computed in the earlier PCA activity?</p></li>
          <li><p>Use the singular values to compute the fraction of total centered variation captured by the first principal direction.</p></li>
        </ol>
        <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5, U6-LO7, U4-LO3 | P+C+M+R | Core]</c></p>
      </statement>
      <solution xml:id="sol-u6-pca-small-data-set-through-svd">
        <p>
          Since <m>n=4</m>,
          <me>
            \sigma_1
            =
            \sqrt{4\lambda_1}
            =
            \sqrt{18}
            =
            3\sqrt2,
            \qquad
            \sigma_2
            =
            \sqrt{4\lambda_2}
            =
            \sqrt2.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          We compute
          <me>
            T=ZV
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3 \amp 1\\
            3 \amp -1\\
            -3 \amp -1\\
            -3 \amp 1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Thus
          <me>
            \mathbf{t}_1
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3\\
            3\\
            -3\\
            -3
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{t}_2
            =
            \frac1{\sqrt2}
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Dividing by the singular values gives
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_1
            =
            \frac12
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1
            \end{bmatrix},
            \qquad
            \mathbf{u}_2
            =
            \frac12
            \begin{bmatrix}
            1\\
            -1\\
            -1\\
            1
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
          Both vectors have norm <m>1</m>, and
          <me>
            \mathbf{u}_1\cdot\mathbf{u}_2=0.
          </me>
          Therefore they are orthonormal.
        </p>
        <p>
          We have
          <me>
            T=U_2\Sigma_2.
          </me>
          Since
          <me>
            T=ZV
          </me>
          and <m>V</m> is orthogonal,
          <me>
            Z
            =
            TV^T
            =
            U_2\Sigma_2V^T.
          </me>
          This factorization uses the two left singular vectors needed to reconstruct <m>Z</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Keeping only the first principal direction gives
          <me>
            \widehat Z_1
            =
            \mathbf{t}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2 \amp 3/2\\
            3/2 \amp 3/2\\
            -3/2 \amp -3/2\\
            -3/2 \amp -3/2
            \end{bmatrix}.
          </me>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each row is the projection of the corresponding centered data vector onto
          <me>
            \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
          </me>
          In particular, the first row is
          <me>
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2 \amp 3/2
            \end{bmatrix},
          </me>
          which is the transpose of the projected centered vector
          <me>
            \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1
            =
            \begin{bmatrix}
            3/2\\
            3/2
            \end{bmatrix}
          </me>
          found in the earlier PCA activity.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally,
          <me>
            \frac{\sigma_1^2}
            {\sigma_1^2+\sigma_2^2}
            =
            \frac{18}{18+2}
            =
            \frac9{10}.
          </me>
          Thus the first principal direction captures <m>90\%</m> of the total centered variation.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </activity>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-u6-apps-computation">
    <title>Applications and computation recap</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        These activities connect absolute extrema, Lagrange multipliers with one or several constraints, quadratic forms, PCA, and SVD. Reopen <xref ref="alg-10-1-finding-maxima-and-minima" text="custom">the absolute-extrema algorithm</xref>, <xref ref="alg-u6-lagrange-one-constraint" text="custom">the one-constraint Lagrange-multiplier algorithm</xref>, <xref ref="alg-u6-lagrange-several-constraints" text="custom">the several-constraint Lagrange-multiplier algorithm</xref>, <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors" text="custom">the fact “Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere”</xref>, <xref ref="def-u6-first-principal-direction" text="custom">the definition “First principal direction”</xref>, or <xref ref="def-u6-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">the definition “Singular value decomposition”</xref> as needed.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-u6-review-activities">
      <title>Review activities</title>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-choosing-candidates-equations">
        <title>Choosing candidates and equations</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each problem below, state the equations used to find candidates and say which candidates must be compared. Do not solve the equations.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute extrema of a differentiable function <m>f(x,y)</m> on the rectangle <m>D=[a,b]\times[c,d]</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>
                  g(\mathbf{x})=k,
                </me>
                assuming <m>\nabla g\ne\mathbf{0}</m> on the feasible set.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>
                  g_1(\mathbf{x})=k_1,
                  \qquad
                  g_2(\mathbf{x})=k_2,
                </me>
                assuming the two constraint gradients are linearly independent on the feasible set.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute extrema of <m>f(\mathbf{x})</m> on the closed bounded region
                <me>
                  D=\{\mathbf{x}:g(\mathbf{x})\leq k\},
                </me>
                assuming <m>\nabla g\ne\mathbf{0}</m> on its boundary.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO1, U6-LO2, U6-LO3 | C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution xml:id="sol-u6-recap-choosing-candidates-equations">
          <p>
            For the rectangle, first solve
            <me>
              \nabla f(x,y)=\mathbf{0}
            </me>
            for critical points in the interior. Restrict <m>f</m> to each of the four edges and find the one-variable critical points on those edges. Include all endpoints and corners. Evaluate <m>f</m> at every candidate and compare the values.
          </p>
          <p>
            With one equality constraint, solve
            <me>
              \nabla f(\mathbf{x})
              =
              \lambda\nabla g(\mathbf{x})
            </me>
            together with
            <me>
              g(\mathbf{x})=k.
            </me>
            Evaluate <m>f</m> at every resulting candidate and compare the values when absolute extrema are requested.
          </p>
          <p>
            With two equality constraints, solve
            <me>
              \nabla f(\mathbf{x})
              =
              \lambda_1\nabla g_1(\mathbf{x})
              +
              \lambda_2\nabla g_2(\mathbf{x})
            </me>
            together with
            <me>
              g_1(\mathbf{x})=k_1,
              \qquad
              g_2(\mathbf{x})=k_2.
            </me>
            Again, evaluate <m>f</m> at all candidates and compare the values.
          </p>
          <p>
            For the region <m>g(\mathbf{x})\leq k</m>, solve
            <me>
              \nabla f(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{0}
            </me>
            at feasible interior points satisfying <m>g(\mathbf{x})&lt;k</m>. On the boundary, solve
            <me>
              \nabla f(\mathbf{x})
              =
              \lambda\nabla g(\mathbf{x}),
              \qquad
              g(\mathbf{x})=k.
            </me>
            Compare all interior and boundary candidates. Since the region is closed and bounded, absolute extrema exist when <m>f</m> is continuous by <xref ref="thm-u6-extreme-value" text="custom">the extreme value theorem</xref>.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-quadratic-form-three-readings">
        <title>One quadratic form, three readings</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              B=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              5 \amp 4\\
              4 \amp 5
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              C=\frac12B.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>B</m> and an orthonormal basis of eigenvectors.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of
                <me>
                  q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}
                </me>
                subject to
                <me>
                  \|\mathbf{x}\|=1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
                <me>
                  A^TA=B.
                </me>
                Find the singular values and right singular directions of <m>A</m>. What is
                <me>
                  \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|?
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Suppose <m>C</m> is the covariance matrix from <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>. Find the first and second principal directions and their captured variations.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why do the same two directions appear in all three readings?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO4, U6-LO5, U6-LO6, U5-LO4 | P+C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution xml:id="sol-u6-recap-quadratic-form-three-readings">
          <p>
            The characteristic polynomial of <m>B</m> is
            <me>
              \det(B-\lambda I)
              =
              (5-\lambda)^2-16
              =
              (\lambda-9)(\lambda-1).
            </me>
            Thus the eigenvalues are
            <me>
              \lambda_1=9,
              \qquad
              \lambda_2=1.
            </me>
            Corresponding unit eigenvectors are
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}_1
              =
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_2
              =
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1\\
              -1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            By <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors" text="custom">the fact “Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere”</xref>,
            <me>
              \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})=9
            </me>
            in direction <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>, while
            <me>
              \min_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}q_B(\mathbf{x})=1
            </me>
            in direction <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            We calculate
            <me>
              A^TA
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 1
              \end{bmatrix}
              \begin{bmatrix}
              1 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 1
              \end{bmatrix}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              5 \amp 4\\
              4 \amp 5
              \end{bmatrix}
              =
              B.
            </me>
            Therefore the singular values of <m>A</m> are
            <me>
              \sigma_1=\sqrt9=3,
              \qquad
              \sigma_2=\sqrt1=1,
            </me>
            and the right singular directions are <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m>. In particular,
            <me>
              \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|=3.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Since
            <me>
              C=\frac12B,
            </me>
            the covariance matrix has the same eigenvectors, with eigenvalues
            <me>
              \frac92
              \qquad\text{and}\qquad
              \frac12.
            </me>
            Thus <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> is a first principal direction and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> is a second principal direction. Their captured variations are <m>9/2</m> and <m>1/2</m>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The first principal direction captures the fraction
            <me>
              \frac{9/2}{9/2+1/2}
              =
              \frac9{10}
            </me>
            of the total centered variation.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same directions appear because the three symmetric matrices being analyzed are
            <me>
              B,\qquad A^TA=B,\qquad C=\frac12B.
            </me>
            Multiplying a matrix by a positive scalar changes its eigenvalues but not its eigenvectors.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-reading-pca-computation">
        <title>Reading a PCA computation</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code repeats part of <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

np.set_printoptions(precision=3, suppress=True)

X = np.array([
    [4.0, 3.0],
    [3.0, 4.0],
    [0.0, 1.0],
    [1.0, 0.0],
])

xbar = X.mean(axis=0)
Z = X - xbar
C = Z.T @ Z / len(X)

eigvals, V = np.linalg.eigh(C)
order = np.argsort(eigvals)[::-1]
eigvals = eigvals[order]
V = V[:, order]

v1 = V[:, 0]
scores1 = Z @ v1
Zhat1 = np.outer(scores1, v1)
R1 = Z - Zhat1

(
    xbar,
    C,
    eigvals,
    V,
    Zhat1,
    np.allclose(R1 @ v1, np.zeros(len(X))),
    V.T @ C @ V,
)
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[(array([2., 2.]),
 array([[2.5, 2. ],
        [2. , 2.5]]),
 array([4.5, 0.5]),
 array([[ 0.707, -0.707],
        [ 0.707,  0.707]]),
 array([[ 1.5,  1.5],
        [ 1.5,  1.5],
        [-1.5, -1.5],
        [-1.5, -1.5]]),
 True,
 array([[4.5, 0. ],
        [0. , 0.5]]))]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Give the shapes of <c>X</c>, <c>xbar</c>, <c>Z</c>, <c>C</c>, <c>V</c>, <c>scores1</c>, and <c>Zhat1</c>.</p></li>
            <li><p>What mathematical objects are stored in <c>xbar</c> and <c>C</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does the code reverse the order returned by <c>np.linalg.eigh</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What do the columns of <c>V</c> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>Interpret <c>scores1</c>, <c>Zhat1</c>, and <c>R1</c>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>What geometric condition is checked by</p>
              <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[np.allclose(R1 @ v1, np.zeros(len(X)))
]]></code></program>
              <p>?</p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Another valid numerical output could replace a column of <c>V</c> by its negative. What would happen to the corresponding scores and reconstructions?</p></li>
            <li><p>Interpret the final diagonal matrix. What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5 | C+R+M+T | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
        <solution xml:id="sol-u6-recap-reading-pca-computation">
          <p>
            The shapes are
            <me>
              X\in\R^{4\times2},
              \qquad
              \bar{\mathbf{x}}\in\R^2,
              \qquad
              Z\in\R^{4\times2},
            </me>
            <me>
              C\in\R^{2\times2},
              \qquad
              V\in\R^{2\times2},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{t}_1\in\R^4,
              \qquad
              \widehat Z_1\in\R^{4\times2}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            The array <c>xbar</c> stores the sample mean
            <me>
              \bar{\mathbf{x}}
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              2\\
              2
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            while <c>C</c> stores the covariance matrix
            <me>
              C=
              \begin{bmatrix}
              5/2 \amp 2\\
              2 \amp 5/2
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <p>
            The command <c>np.linalg.eigh</c> returns the eigenvalues in increasing order. The reversal places the largest eigenvalue first. The first column of <m>V</m> is therefore a first principal direction, and the second column is a second principal direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            The vector <c>scores1</c> stores
            <me>
              \mathbf{t}_1=Z\mathbf{v}_1.
            </me>
            The rows of <c>Zhat1</c> are the projections of the centered data vectors onto
            <me>
              \spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.
            </me>
            The matrix <c>R1</c> stores the corresponding reconstruction residuals.
          </p>
          <p>
            The product <c>R1 @ v1</c> computes the dot product of each residual row with <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The value <c>True</c> checks that every reconstruction residual is numerically perpendicular to the first principal direction.
          </p>
          <p>
            Replacing <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> by <m>-\mathbf{v}_1</m> reverses the signs of its scores. However,
            <me>
              (-t_i)(-\mathbf{v}_1)=t_i\mathbf{v}_1,
            </me>
            so the projected points and reconstruction residuals do not change. The principal axis is the same.
          </p>
          <p>
            The final output says that covariance in the principal-coordinate system is
            <me>
              V^TCV
              =
              \begin{bmatrix}
              4.5 \amp 0\\
              0 \amp 0.5
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            The diagonal entries are the captured variations in the two principal directions. The first direction captures
            <me>
              \frac{4.5}{4.5+0.5}
              =
              \frac9{10}
            </me>
            of the total centered variation.
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-svd-shapes">
        <title>Reading SVD shapes</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The following code computes a reduced SVD.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[import numpy as np

A = np.array([[ 1., -1.],
              [-2.,  2.],
              [ 2., -2.]])

U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(A, full_matrices=False)

A.shape, U.shape, s.shape, Vt.shape
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[((3, 2), (3, 2), (2,), (2, 2))]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>What is the shape of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>How many singular values are returned?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why is <c>s.shape</c> equal to <c>(2,)</c> rather than <c>(2, 2)</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What matrix does <c>Vt</c> represent?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>full_matrices=False</c> do in this example?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The matrix <m>A</m> is <m>3\times 2</m>. The reduced SVD returns two singular values because
            <me>\min(3,2)=2.</me>
            The array <c>s</c> stores only the diagonal entries of <m>\Sigma</m>, not the full diagonal matrix, so its shape is <c>(2,)</c>. The array <c>Vt</c> represents <m>V^T</m>. With <c>full_matrices=False</c>, NumPy returns the reduced SVD shapes needed for reconstruction:
            <me>
              U\in\mathbb R^{3\times 2},
              \qquad
              s\in\mathbb R^2,
              \qquad
              V^T\in\mathbb R^{2\times 2}.
            </me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>

      <activity xml:id="act-u6-recap-singular-vector-identity">
        <title>Checking a singular-vector identity in code</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For an SVD
            <me>A=U\Sigma V^T,</me>
            the identity
            <me>A\mathbf{v}_i=\sigma_i\mathbf{u}_i</me>
            is one of the main ways to read the factors. The following code checks this identity for a diagonal stretch.
          </p>
          <program language="python"><code><![CDATA[A = np.array([[3., 0.],
              [0., 1.]])

U, s, Vt = np.linalg.svd(A, full_matrices=False)

i = 0
v = Vt.T[:, i]
lhs = A @ v
rhs = s[i] * U[:, i]

lhs, rhs, np.allclose(lhs, rhs)
]]></code></program>
          <p>Output:</p>
          <pre><![CDATA[(array([3., 0.]), array([3., 0.]), True)]]></pre>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Which vector is stored in <c>v</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>lhs</c> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does <c>rhs</c> compute?</p></li>
            <li><p>Why does <c>np.allclose(lhs, rhs)</c> return <c>True</c>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which stretch factor appears in this computation?</p></li>
          </ol>
        </statement>
        <solution>
          <p>
            The vector <c>v</c> is the first right singular vector <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The array <c>lhs</c> computes <m>A\mathbf{v}_1</m>. The array <c>rhs</c> computes <m>\sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1</m>. The value <c>True</c> means that the numerical computation agrees with
            <me>A\mathbf{v}_1=\sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1.</me>
            The stretch factor is
            <me>\sigma_1=3.</me>
          </p>
        </solution>
      </activity>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="lab-u6-callout">
      <title>Linked notebook</title>
      <p>
        Run <url href="https://sebroc.github.io/MATH345-Course-Materials/labs/#lab-u6">Lab U6: Constraints, PCA, and SVD</url>. The lab checks one- and several-constraint Lagrange conditions, connects quadratic forms with maximum stretch, computes PCA from covariance and from the SVD, compares PCA with linear regression, and reads rank and the four fundamental subspaces from an SVD.
      </p>
    </subsection>
    <conclusion>
      <p>
        For a quick reference on arrays and shapes, matrix products, numerical checks, eigenvalue and SVD commands, and plotting, see the programming appendix sections <xref ref="appcomp-arrays-shapes" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-elementwise-vs-linear-algebra" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numerical-checks" text="global"/>, <xref ref="appcomp-numpy-linalg" text="global"/>, and <xref ref="appcomp-plotting" text="global"/>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u6-exercises">
    <title>Exercises</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Exercise tags.</em> The tags below identify the primary outcomes and competency types for each exercise group. Individual problems may also involve earlier outcomes.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-exercises-bridge-lagrange-parallel-gradients">
      <title>Lagrange multipliers</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U6-LO1, U6-LO2. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C+M.</p>
      <p><url href="https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-3/pages/4-8-lagrange-multipliers">OpenStax 4.8</url> exercises: 358, 360, 362, 364, 366, 368, 370, 372, 374, 376, 378, 380, 382, 384, 386, 388, 390, and 392.</p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-exercises-sec-singular-value-decomposition">
      <title>Singular value decomposition</title>
      <p><em>Primary outcomes.</em> U6-LO4, U6-LO6, U6-LO7. <em>Competencies.</em> P+C.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><url href="https://math.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Linear_Algebra/Linear_Algebra_with_Applications_(Nicholson)/08%3A_Orthogonality/8.06%3A_The_Singular_Value_Decomposition/8.6E%3A_The_Singular_Value_Decomposition_Exercises">Nicholson 8.6.9(b)</url></p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="u6-exercises-applied-computational">
      <title>Additional applied and computational problems</title>
      <p>
        These exercises connect absolute extrema, Lagrange multipliers with one and several constraints, quadratic forms, maximum stretch, PCA, SVD, rank, and the four fundamental subspaces. Solution sketches are collected in Appendix <xref ref="app-u6-applied-solutions" text="global"/>.
      </p>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-candidate-table-rectangle">
        <title>Candidate table on a rectangle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-2x+4y</me>
            on the rectangle
            <me>D=\{(x,y):0\leq x\leq 3,\ -3\leq y\leq 1\}.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Find the critical point of <m>f</m> in the interior of <m>D</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the boundary candidates on each of the four edges.</p></li>
            <li><p>Include the four corner points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Make a candidate table with the source of each candidate and the value of <m>f</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the absolute maximum and absolute minimum of <m>f</m> on <m>D</m>.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO1 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-parallel-gradients-circle">
        <title>Parallel gradients on a circle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y)=3x+4y</me>
            and constrain the input to the circle
            <me>x^2+y^2=25.</me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Write the constraint as <m>g(x,y)=25</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Solve
                <me>\nabla f=\lambda\nabla g.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Find the constrained critical points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Evaluate <m>f</m> at the constrained critical points.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which point gives the absolute maximum? Which point gives the absolute minimum?</p></li>
            <li><p>Explain geometrically why <m>\nabla f</m> is parallel to <m>\nabla g</m> at those points.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO2 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-quadratic-form-unit-circle">
        <title>Quadratic form on the unit circle</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              B=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                5\amp 0\\
                0\amp 2
              \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            and
            <me>q(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}.</me>
            Write
            <me>
              \mathbf{x}=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                x\\
                y
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Write <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> in terms of <m>x</m> and <m>y</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Maximize <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Minimize <m>q(\mathbf{x})</m> subject to
                <me>\|\mathbf{x}\|=1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which unit vectors give the extreme values?</p></li>
            <li><p>How do the answers relate to eigenvalues and eigenvectors of <m>B</m>?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO4 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-maximum-stretch-matrix">
        <title>Maximum stretch of a matrix</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>
              A=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 2\\
                2\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Compute <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find the singular values of <m>A</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Find a unit right singular vector corresponding to the largest singular value.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What is
                <me>\max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|?</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which input direction is stretched most?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO4 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-reading-svd">
        <title>Reading an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Suppose
            <me>A=U\Sigma V^T,</me>
            where
            <me>
              U=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 0\\
                0\amp 1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \Sigma=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                5\amp 0\amp 0\\
                0\amp 2\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              V=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1/\sqrt2\amp 0\amp 1/\sqrt2\\
                1/\sqrt2\amp 0\amp -1/\sqrt2\\
                0\amp 1\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            Thus the right singular vectors are
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}_1=
              \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1\\
                0
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                0\\
                0\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{v}_3=
              \frac{1}{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                -1\\
                0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>What are the singular values of <m>A</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What is <m>\operatorname{rank}(A)</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>Compute <m>A\mathbf{v}_1</m>, <m>A\mathbf{v}_2</m>, and <m>A\mathbf{v}_3</m> using the SVD.</p></li>
            <li><p>Which input direction is forgotten?</p></li>
            <li><p>Which input directions are transmitted?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO6, U6-LO7 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-fundamental-subspaces-svd">
        <title>Fundamental subspaces from an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the SVD data from the previous checkpoint.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{row}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{null}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{col}(A)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Give an orthonormal basis for <m>\operatorname{null}(A^T)</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Check that the dimensions agree with rank-nullity.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO7 | C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-two-constraints-sphere">
        <title>Two constraints on a sphere</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let
            <me>f(x,y,z)=x-y.</me>
          </p>
          <p>
            Find the absolute extrema of <m>f</m> subject to
            <me>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</me>
            and
            <me>x+y+z=0.</me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Describe the feasible set geometrically. Why do absolute extrema exist?</p></li>
            <li><p>Show that the gradients of the two constraints are linearly independent at every feasible point.</p></li>
            <li><p>Form the Lagrangian using multipliers <m>\lambda</m> and <m>\mu</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p>Write and solve the Lagrange equations.</p></li>
            <li><p>Evaluate <m>f</m> at the candidates and identify the absolute maximum and minimum.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO3 | P+C+R | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data">
        <title>Principal directions of a centered data set</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            The rows of
            <me>
              X=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                4\amp2\\
                2\amp4\\
                -2\amp0\\
                0\amp-2
              \end{bmatrix}
            </me>
            are four data points in <m>\R^2</m>.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><p>Compute the mean <m>\bar{\mathbf{x}}</m> and the centered data matrix <m>Z</m>.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the covariance matrix
                <me>C=\frac14Z^TZ.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Find the eigenvalues of <m>C</m> and corresponding orthonormal eigenvectors. Choose <m>\mathbf{v}_1</m> for the larger eigenvalue and <m>\mathbf{v}_2</m> for the smaller eigenvalue.</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Form
                <me>
                  V=
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    \mathbf{v}_1\amp\mathbf{v}_2
                  \end{bmatrix}
                </me>
                and compute the principal-coordinate matrix
                <me>T=ZV.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute
                <me>V^TCV.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5 | P+C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-pca-reconstruction-regression">
        <title>PCA reconstruction and regression</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Use the data and principal directions from <xref ref="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data" text="custom">the checkpoint “Principal directions of a centered data set”</xref>. The first centered data vector is
            <me>
              \mathbf{z}_1=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                3\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              \mathbf{v}_1
              =
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the first principal-component score
                <me>t_1=\mathbf{v}_1^T\mathbf{z}_1.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the projected centered vector
                <me>\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1=t_1\mathbf{v}_1</me>
                and the reconstruction residual
                <me>
                  \mathbf{r}_1
                  =
                  \mathbf{z}_1-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Check that
                <me>\mathbf{r}_1\cdot\mathbf{v}_1=0.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Find the reconstructed original data point
                <me>
                  \widehat{\mathbf{x}}_1
                  =
                  \bar{\mathbf{x}}+\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_1.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Give an equation for the PCA line
                <me>\bar{\mathbf{x}}+\spans\{\mathbf{v}_1\}.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                For the regression of <m>y</m> on <m>x</m>, use
                <me>
                  m=\frac{C_{12}}{C_{11}},
                  \qquad
                  b=\bar y-m\bar x
                </me>
                to find the regression line.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Why are the PCA and regression lines different? Describe the residual direction minimized by each method.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5, U4-LO5 | P+C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-redundant-features-singular-values">
        <title>Redundant features revisited by singular values</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Return to the Unit 2 redundant-feature matrix
            <me>
              X=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp 2\amp 3\\
                2\amp 4\amp 6\\
                0\amp 1\amp 1\\
                1\amp -1\amp 0
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
            The third column is the sum of the first two columns.
          </p>
          <ol marker="a">
            <li>
              <p>
                Verify that
                <me>
                  X
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    1\\
                    1\\
                    -1
                  \end{bmatrix}
                  =
                  \mathbf{0}.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What does this say about <m>\operatorname{null}(X)</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What does this say about the rank of <m>X</m>?</p></li>
            <li><p>What singular value should appear because of this redundancy?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                If <m>X\mathbf{c}=\mathbf{y}</m>, what is
                <me>
                  X\left(\mathbf{c}+t
                  \begin{bmatrix}
                    1\\
                    1\\
                    -1
                  \end{bmatrix}\right)?
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Explain how this revisits the Unit 2 idea of nonunique coefficient vectors.</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U2-LO6, U6-LO7 | C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-u6-checkpoint-pca-from-svd">
        <title>PCA from an SVD</title>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Return to the centered data matrix from <xref ref="ex-u6-checkpoint-principal-directions-centered-data" text="custom">the checkpoint “Principal directions of a centered data set”</xref>. Suppose
            <me>
              Z=U_2\Sigma_2V^T,
            </me>
            where
            <me>
              \mathbf{u}_1
              =
              \frac12
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                1\\
                -1\\
                -1
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \mathbf{u}_2
              =
              \frac12
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\\
                -1\\
                -1\\
                1
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            <me>
              U_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                \mathbf{u}_1\amp\mathbf{u}_2
              \end{bmatrix},
              \qquad
              \Sigma_2=
              \begin{bmatrix}
                4\sqrt2\amp0\\
                0\amp2\sqrt2
              \end{bmatrix},
            </me>
            and
            <me>
              V=
              \frac1{\sqrt2}
              \begin{bmatrix}
                1\amp1\\
                1\amp-1
              \end{bmatrix}.
            </me>
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use
                <me>\lambda_j=\frac{\sigma_j^2}{4}</me>
                to recover the two covariance eigenvalues.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>Which columns of <m>V</m> are the first and second principal directions?</p></li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the principal-coordinate matrix
                <me>T=U_2\Sigma_2.</me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute the reconstruction using only the first principal direction:
                <me>
                  \widehat Z_1
                  =
                  \sigma_1\mathbf{u}_1\mathbf{v}_1^T.
                </me>
              </p>
            </li>
            <li><p>What fraction of the total centered variation is captured by the first principal direction?</p></li>
            <li><p>What do the two columns of <m>T</m> represent?</p></li>
          </ol>
          <p><em>Tags.</em> <c>[U6-LO5, U6-LO7 | C+R+M | Core]</c></p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="u6-highlights">
    <title>Unit 6 highlights</title>
    <p><em>Big questions.</em> How do we find absolute extrema when the allowable inputs are restricted? Why do eigenvectors identify extremal directions of quadratic forms, centered data, and matrix maps?</p>
    <p><em>Notation.</em></p>
    <me>
      S=\{\mathbf{x}:g(\mathbf{x})=k\},
      \qquad
      \nabla f=\lambda\nabla g,
    </me>
    <me>
      \nabla f
      =
      \lambda_1\nabla g_1+\cdots+\lambda_m\nabla g_m,
      \qquad
      L(\mathbf{x},\lambda)
      =
      f(\mathbf{x})-\lambda\bigl(g(\mathbf{x})-k\bigr),
    </me>
    <me>
      q_B(\mathbf{x})=\mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x},
      \qquad
      \|\mathbf{x}\|=1,
      \qquad
      \|A\mathbf{x}\|^2=\mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x},
    </me>
    <me>
      \mathbf{z}_i=\mathbf{x}_i-\bar{\mathbf{x}},
      \qquad
      C=\frac1nZ^TZ,
      \qquad
      \mathbf{t}=Z\mathbf{v},
    </me>
    <me>
      \widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i=t_i\mathbf{v},
      \qquad
      \mathbf{r}_i=\mathbf{z}_i-\widehat{\mathbf{z}}_i,
      \qquad
      V^TCV=\operatorname{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_d),
    </me>
    <me>
      A=U\Sigma V^T,
      \qquad
      A\mathbf{v}_i=\sigma_i\mathbf{u}_i,
      \qquad
      T=ZV=U\Sigma,
      \qquad
      \lambda_i=\frac{\sigma_i^2}{n},
    </me>
    <p>and</p>
    <me>
      \operatorname{row}(A),\quad
      \operatorname{col}(A),\quad
      \operatorname{null}(A),\quad
      \operatorname{null}(A^T).
    </me>
    <p><em>Concepts.</em> Closed bounded region, interior candidate, boundary candidate, absolute extremum, feasible set, equality constraint, constrained critical point, Lagrange multiplier, Lagrangian, tangent direction, normal direction, independent constraint gradients, symmetric quadratic form, principal axes, maximum stretch, centered data, covariance matrix, principal direction, principal-component score, reconstruction, reconstruction residual, captured variation, singular value, right singular vector, left singular vector, singular value decomposition, row space, column space, null space, and left null space.</p>
    <p><em>Results.</em> By <xref ref="thm-u6-extreme-value" text="custom">the extreme value theorem</xref>, a continuous function on a closed bounded region has absolute extrema. <xref ref="alg-10-1-finding-maxima-and-minima" text="custom">The absolute-extrema algorithm</xref> finds them by comparing all interior and boundary candidates.</p>
    <p>At a constrained local extremum with one equality constraint and</p>
    <me>
      \nabla g(\mathbf{a})\ne\mathbf{0},
    </me>
    <p><xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier" text="custom">the one-constraint Lagrange multiplier theorem</xref> gives</p>
    <me>
      \nabla f(\mathbf{a})
      =
      \lambda\nabla g(\mathbf{a}).
    </me>
    <p>With several constraints whose gradients are linearly independent, <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier-several-constraints" text="custom">the several-constraint Lagrange multiplier theorem</xref> gives</p>
    <me>
      \nabla f(\mathbf{a})
      =
      \lambda_1\nabla g_1(\mathbf{a})
      +\cdots+
      \lambda_m\nabla g_m(\mathbf{a}).
    </me>
    <p>The Lagrangian packages the gradient equation and the constraint equations into one critical-point system.</p>
    <p>For a symmetric matrix <m>B</m>, <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors" text="custom">the fact “Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere”</xref> says that the largest and smallest values of</p>
    <me>
      \mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}
    </me>
    <p>on the unit sphere are the largest and smallest eigenvalues of <m>B</m>. Applying this result to <m>A^TA</m> identifies the right singular directions of <m>A</m>, and the largest singular value is</p>
    <me>
      \max_{\|\mathbf{x}\|=1}\|A\mathbf{x}\|.
    </me>
    <p>For centered data, principal directions may be chosen as orthonormal eigenvectors of the covariance matrix, ordered from largest to smallest eigenvalue. Maximizing total squared scores is equivalent to minimizing total squared reconstruction residuals. Principal coordinates diagonalize the covariance matrix. PCA and linear regression use different residuals and can therefore produce different best-fit lines.</p>
    <p>By <xref ref="theorem-svd" text="custom">the theorem “Every real matrix has an SVD”</xref>, every real matrix has a factorization</p>
    <me>
      A=U\Sigma V^T.
    </me>
    <p>The right singular vectors give orthonormal input directions, the singular values give nonnegative stretch factors, and the left singular vectors give orthonormal output directions. By <xref ref="thm-u6-fundamental-subspaces-svd" text="custom">the theorem “Fundamental subspaces from an SVD”</xref>, the positive and zero singular directions give orthonormal bases for the four fundamental subspaces, and the number of positive singular values is <m>\operatorname{rank}(A)</m>.</p>
    <p>Finally, <xref ref="fact-u6-pca-from-centered-data-svd" text="custom">the fact “PCA from a centered-data SVD”</xref> gives</p>
    <me>
      C
      =
      V\left(\frac1n\Sigma^T\Sigma\right)V^T,
      \qquad
      T=ZV=U\Sigma.
    </me>
    <p>Thus the right singular vectors of centered data are principal directions, while the columns of <m>U\Sigma</m> are principal-component score vectors.</p>
    <p><em>Toolbox skills.</em> Build and compare candidate tables; restrict functions to simple boundary pieces; form and solve one- and several-constraint Lagrange systems; write and differentiate Lagrangians; evaluate and diagonalize symmetric quadratic forms; connect eigenvalues with extrema on the unit sphere; center data and form covariance matrices; compute principal directions, scores, reconstructions, residuals, and captured-variation fractions; distinguish PCA from regression; form <m>A^TA</m>; compute singular values and singular vectors; assemble and read small SVDs; check</p>
    <me>
      A\mathbf{v}_i=\sigma_i\mathbf{u}_i;
    </me>
    <p>and identify rank and bases for the four fundamental subspaces.</p>
    <p><em>Main applications.</em> Absolute extrema under geometric, budget, resource, or unit-length constraints; principal directions and lower-dimensional descriptions of centered data; reconstruction of data from principal-component scores; comparison of PCA with linear regression; maximum stretch and forgotten directions of a matrix map; and orthonormal descriptions of row, column, and null spaces.</p>
    <p><em>Connections.</em> Unit 1 supplies vectors, matrix maps, matrix-vector products, coordinate directions, and shape checks. Unit 2 supplies rank, row and column spaces, null spaces, reachable outputs, forgotten input directions, and nonunique coefficient vectors. Unit 3 supplies level sets, gradients, tangent directions, and closed and bounded sets.</p>
    <p>Unit 4 supplies <xref ref="def-orthogonal-projection" text="custom">the definition of orthogonal projection</xref> and <xref ref="thm-6-2projection" text="custom">the projection theorem</xref>. PCA first chooses a subspace and then projects centered data onto it. Linear regression instead projects a response vector onto the column space of a design matrix.</p>
    <p>Unit 5 supplies critical points, eigenvalues, eigenvectors, symmetric matrices, quadratic forms, and <xref ref="thm-principal-axes" text="custom">the principal axes theorem</xref>. Lagrange multipliers give an optimization interpretation of the same eigenvector directions. Unit 7 reuses orthogonality, projection, and quadratic forms in polynomial and inner-product spaces.</p>
    <p><em>Study anchors.</em> U6-LO1: <xref ref="sec-absolute-maxima" text="custom">the section “Absolute extrema on closed bounded regions”</xref> and <xref ref="alg-10-1-finding-maxima-and-minima" text="custom">the absolute-extrema algorithm</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO2: <xref ref="bridge-lagrange-parallel-gradients" text="custom">the definition “Constrained optimization with one equality constraint”</xref>, <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier" text="custom">the one-constraint Lagrange multiplier theorem</xref>, and <xref ref="alg-u6-lagrange-one-constraint" text="custom">the one-constraint Lagrange-multiplier algorithm</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO3: <xref ref="def-u6-constrained-critical-point-several-constraints" text="custom">the definition “Constrained critical point with several constraints”</xref>, <xref ref="thm-u6-lagrange-multiplier-several-constraints" text="custom">the several-constraint Lagrange multiplier theorem</xref>, and <xref ref="alg-u6-lagrange-several-constraints" text="custom">the several-constraint Lagrange-multiplier algorithm</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO4: <xref ref="sec-maximum-stretch-svd" text="custom">the section “Quadratic forms and maximum stretch”</xref> and <xref ref="fact-u6-maximum-stretch-eigenvectors" text="custom">the fact “Extrema of a symmetric quadratic form on the unit sphere”</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO5: <xref ref="sec-u6-principal-component-analysis" text="custom">the section “Principal component analysis”</xref>, <xref ref="def-u6-first-principal-direction" text="custom">the definition “First principal direction”</xref>, <xref ref="def-u6-second-principal-direction" text="custom">the definition “Second principal direction”</xref>, and <xref ref="act-u6-pca-small-data-set" text="custom">the activity “Principal directions of a small data set”</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO6: <xref ref="sec-singular-value-decomposition" text="custom">the section “Singular value decomposition”</xref>, <xref ref="theorem-svd" text="custom">the theorem “Every real matrix has an SVD”</xref>, and <xref ref="activity-svd-assembling-factorization" text="custom">the activity “Checking a completed SVD”</xref>.</p>
    <p>U6-LO7: <xref ref="thm-u6-fundamental-subspaces-svd" text="custom">the theorem “Fundamental subspaces from an SVD”</xref>, <xref ref="sec-u6-svd-information-channels" text="custom">the section “PCA from the SVD”</xref>, and <xref ref="fact-u6-pca-from-centered-data-svd" text="custom">the fact “PCA from a centered-data SVD”</xref>.</p>
    <p>Code interpretation and mixed review: <xref ref="sec-u6-apps-computation" text="custom">the section “Applications and computation recap”</xref>.</p>
    <p><em>Applications and computations readiness checklist.</em> Given a short Unit 6 calculation, code snippet, table, or diagram, I can decide whether interior and boundary candidates are needed; compare candidate values; set up one- or several-constraint Lagrange equations; include every constraint equation; explain the parallel-gradient and linear-combination conditions; differentiate a Lagrangian; and recognize when the hypotheses on the constraint gradients fail.</p>
    <p>I can connect extrema of</p>
    <me>
      \mathbf{x}^TB\mathbf{x}
    </me>
    <p>on the unit sphere with eigenvalues and eigenvectors; read</p>
    <me>
      \|A\mathbf{x}\|^2
      =
      \mathbf{x}^TA^TA\mathbf{x}
    </me>
    <p>as a maximum-stretch calculation; center a data matrix; form and interpret a covariance matrix; compute principal directions and scores; reconstruct centered data; check residual orthogonality; compute the fraction of variation captured; and distinguish PCA’s perpendicular reconstruction residuals from regression’s vertical prediction residuals.</p>
    <p>I can interpret <c>X.mean(axis=0)</c>, <c>Z.T @ Z / n</c>, <c>np.linalg.eigh(C)</c>, <c>Z @ v</c>, and <c>np.outer(scores, v)</c>; explain eigenvector ordering and sign changes; read the shapes of <m>U</m>, <c>s</c>, and <c>Vt</c> from a reduced NumPy SVD; check</p>
    <me>
      A\mathbf{v}_i=\sigma_i\mathbf{u}_i;
    </me>
    <p>read rank from positive singular values; identify the four fundamental subspaces from <m>U</m> and <m>V</m>; and connect covariance eigenvalues with squared singular values of centered data.</p>
    <p><em>Common mistakes.</em> Ignoring boundary candidates; treating constrained critical points as final answers without comparing objective values; forgetting one or more constraint equations; writing</p>
    <me>
      \nabla f=\lambda\nabla f
    </me>
    <p>instead of using the constraint gradient; applying the usual Lagrange theorem where a constraint gradient is zero or where several constraint gradients are dependent; assuming that the objective gradient must be parallel to each gradient in a several-constraint problem; or assuming that a Lagrange multiplier is always the objective value.</p>
    <p>Other common mistakes are optimizing a quadratic form or PCA objective without imposing unit length; failing to center data before forming the covariance matrix; choosing the smallest covariance eigenvalue for the first principal direction; treating <m>\mathbf{v}</m> and <m>-\mathbf{v}</m> as different principal axes; confusing a score <m>t_i</m>, a reconstructed vector <m>t_i\mathbf{v}</m>, and a residual <m>\mathbf{r}_i</m>; treating PCA residuals as vertical or regression residuals as perpendicular; confusing eigenvalues of <m>A^TA</m> with singular values of <m>A</m>; confusing <m>U</m>, <m>V</m>, and <m>V^T</m>; expecting reduced NumPy SVD factors to have the full-SVD shapes; assigning right singular vectors to output spaces or left singular vectors to input spaces; or treating a small numerical singular value as automatically equal to zero.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
